The choice of loss function in a deep learning model has been largely based on intuition, with limited empirical support to guide this critical decision. Our study investigates the impact of loss function selection in a classification setting, examining the effects on performance, training stability, and generalization across multiple networks and datasets. Our findings reveal that modifying the standard loss function with additional penalties has both benefits and drawbacks, with the particular advantage of using the target class's cross-entropy loss among the notable outcomes. To facilitate reproducibility, our code and data are available for download at the specified link. Experimental results demonstrate that the particular advantage of using the target class's cross-entropy loss. Additionally, our analysis points to several promising research directions in loss function assessment. Our code and data are available for download at the specified link. 

