In the past few decades, the way data is structured within musical recordings has undergone significant changes. To quantify this transformation, researchers have proposed using data complexity measures, such as Pearson correlation information and random forest importance, to assess how audio is represented. However, these methods rely on explicit annotation of the data, which is not available for all recording types. To address this limitation, this study focuses on annotated musical datasets from the UK between 1980 and 2019 to analyze the evolution of audio organization. Our results show a noticeable shift in audio arrangement, with the prominence of instruments and musical sections varying over time. Interestingly, the most significant changes observed are in the mid-1990s and following the millennium change. To contextualize these findings, wavelet-based feature extraction is used to identify underlying trends in audio representation. Our investigation enables new insights into audio organization and suggests various future research directions in this area. Our dataset and analysis are available for download at the specified link. 

